.TH ranlib "" "Command"
.SH NAME
ranlib \- create index for library
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBranlib \fIlibrary ...\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
ranlib -- create index for library
Usage:	ranlib library ...
.HE
.B ranlib
processes each named
.I library
(an object archive as produced by
.BR ar )
so that the loader can search it quickly: it reads every module in
the archive, collects the names of its defined global symbols, and
writes them, together with the byte offset of the module that
defines each one, into a header file.
.B ranlib
then invokes
.B ar
(via
.BR system() )
to insert or replace that header as a module named
.B __.SYMDEF
at the front of the archive, overwriting any
.B __.SYMDEF
already there.
.PP
.B ranlib
is itself a standalone program, not a mode of
.BR ar ;
it drives
.B ar
as a subprocess to do the actual archive surgery, after building the
symbol table itself by reading each member's load-module header and
symbol table directly.
.SH FILES
.DS
.ta 14n
\fB__.SYMDEF\fR	symbol-index module written at the front of the archive
.DE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ar ,
.B ld
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B ranlib
complains if a named archive cannot be opened, if it cannot create
its header file, or if a member of the archive is not a valid load
module.
.B ranlib
processes each named
.I library
independently; its exit status is the number of libraries in which
an error was reported.
